      "type": "numerical_input",
      "question": "If 9^(x+1) = 27^(x−1), what is x?",
      "answer": {
        "value": 5,
        "tolerance": 0
      },
      "explanation": "Write both sides in base 3: 3^(2(x+1)) = 3^(3(x−1)). So 2x + 2 = 3x − 3 and x = 5. Check: 9⁶ = 531,441 = 27⁴.",
      "solutionSteps": [
        "9 = 3² and 27 = 3³.",
        "2x + 2 = 3x − 3.",
        "x = 5."
      ],
      "formulaIds": [
        "si-equation-f"
      ],
      "method": "Common base, then equate exponents.",
      "hints": [
        {
          "level": 1,
          "text": "Both 9 and 27 are powers of 3."
        }
      ],
      "commonMistakes": [
        "Equating exponents before changing to a common base."
      ],
      "tags": [
        "two-step"
      ],
      "estimatedSeconds": 60,
